Pointer
A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable.

Pointer Variable
A pointer variable is a variable used to store the address of another variable.

Address-of Operator (&)
The & operator is used to get the memory address of a variable.

Dereference Operator (*)
The * operator is used to access the value stored at a memory address.

Pointer Declaration
Pointer declaration defines a pointer variable that can store an address.
Example:
int *p;


Pointer Initialization
Pointer initialization means assigning the address of a variable to a pointer.
Example:
int a = 10;
int *p = &a;


NULL Pointer
A NULL pointer is a pointer that does not point to any valid memory location.
int *p = NULL;


Pointer to Pointer
A pointer to pointer is a pointer that stores the address of another pointer.
int **p;


Pointer Arithmetic
Pointer arithmetic means performing operations such as addition and subtraction on pointers.

Pointer to Array
A pointer to an array is a pointer that stores the address of an array element or array.

Pointer to Function
A function pointer is a pointer that stores the address of a function.

Pointer to Structure
A structure pointer is a pointer that stores the address of a structure variable.

Wild Pointer
A wild pointer is an uninitialized pointer that contains an unknown address.

Dangling Pointer
A dangling pointer is a pointer that refers to memory that is no longer valid.

Void Pointer
A void pointer is a pointer that can store the address of any data type.